<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mijikenda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mijikenda, meaning nine towns, are a group of nine related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples of the Kenyan coastal hinterland, from the Tanzanian border north towards Malindi. Living traditionally in fortified hilltop villages called kaya, they traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, grain and other produce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns and allied with the Mazrui of Mombasa. Their sacred kaya forests are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
